Comment:
1) Schedule F carries the risk of unwarranted intrusion on merit-based review of science thereby limiting the ability to conduct science without bias.
2) Peer review for NIH & other agencies is mandated by federal law;
3) Program officers at NIH, DOD, NSF & other agencies should not become Schedule F employees.

This is a blatant attempt to subvert the will of the people and make science weak:

1) Schedule F carries the risk of unwarranted intrusion on merit-based review of science;
2) Peer review for NIH & other agencies is mandated by federal law;
3) Program officers at NIH, DOD, NSF & other agencies should not become Schedule F employees.

The Benefits section asserts that faster removals will create “large gains in agency efficiency” but admits OPM “does not have a reasonable basis for estimating the magnitude.” E.O. 12866 requires the agency to quantify benefits where feasible.  At minimum, OPM can draw on:

  - MSPB survey data on supervisor time lost to poor performers;  
  - FDIC Special Counsel report quantifying misconduct-related costs;  
  - GAO’s 2015 estimate ($350 million/yr) of “desk-duty” employees.

Why were these readily available sources ignored?  Absent monetized benefits, OPM cannot show the rule is cost-justified, nor that it qualifies as an E.O. 14192 “deregulatory action.”  The final rule must supply concrete dollar values or explain, with record evidence, why monetization is impossible.

Greater federal workforce turnover, with changes in presidential administrations ushering in new cadres of policy-influencing employees, causing increased instability in agencies’ interpretations and implementations of laws;
Decline in the institutional history and technical expertise career civil servants develop and bring to bear in formulating policy;
Less accountability to Congress regarding the laws that agencies administer; and
More opportunities for political favoritism in federal contracting.

The title of the proposed Schedule F rule is a misnomer, as it does nothing to improve performance, accountability or responsiveness. It is a loyalty test, and not to the constitution or the people, but to a political person.  At an agency level, employees are generally non-political in performing their duties.  They adjust to the policy objectives of each administration, but are not loyal to an administration's politics.
As a former federal employee, it has been my experience that people hired as a personal, political, or fraternal favor have demonstrated the following: 
They are less motivated to perform;
They are promoted and paid at a level they are not qualified for;
They do not have basic institutional knowledge and cannot interact effectively;
They do not have the requisite critical thinking skills;
They do not have the type of management experience that applies to the mission of the agency;
They are more likely to apply personal biases;
They are generally perceived with suspicion for underlying agenda or favoritism that is antithetical to the mission of the agency;
They are less meticulous in following policy, rules, regulations, and laws, as they have a benefactor protecting them.

As a member of the vision research community and a supporter of evidence-based public policy, I write in strong opposition to the proposed rule that would enable the reclassification of federal employees into a new Schedule F category.
This proposed rule would severely jeopardize the integrity and continuity of scientific research across the federal government, including at the National Eye Institute (NEI), where apolitical, expert leadership is essential to advancing breakthroughs in vision science and protecting the eye health of millions of Americans. Reclassifying career scientists, grant program staff, and policy experts under Schedule F would make it easier to dismiss qualified personnel for political reasons and insert partisan appointees into roles that should be guided solely by science and merit and that should maintain continuity from Administration to Administration.
Vision research is a nonpartisan national priority. It leads to the development of new diagnostics, treatments, and cures for conditions that affect nearly 100 million Americans and cost the U.S. economy billions annually. The progress vision researchers have made—from gene therapies that restore sight to AI-driven diagnostics that detect systemic disease through the eye—has only been possible because of the long-term stability, independence, and credibility of federal science agencies, career staff, and the peer-review grant-making process.
Introducing political instability through Schedule F would:
•	Undermine the merit-based grant review process at NIH, including NEI;
•	Threaten evidence-based policymaking in public health and scientific agencies;
•	Disrupt career pathways for young scientists entering government service;
•	Cause significant staff attrition and institutional knowledge loss at federal agencies.
Scientific research thrives in an environment of stability, transparency, and nonpartisan governance. Schedule F would do the opposite, creating turnover, chilling scientific integrity, and injecting political influence where it does not belong.
I respectfully urge you to abandon the implementation of Schedule F for these employees at NIH and other agencies to protect the integrity of our nation's scientific workforce.

