This proposed rule would severely jeopardize the integrity and continuity of scientific research across the federal government, including at the National Eye Institute (NEI), where expert leadership is essential to advancing breakthroughs in vision science and protecting the eye health of millions of Americans. All of the program officers I’ve worked with at NEI are competent experts who interpret feedback from reviewers and provide expert advice to applicants about the connection of their work to current NIH priorities. Reclassifying career scientists, grant program staff, and policy experts under Schedule F would make it easier to dismiss qualified experts for unqualified appointees. These critical roles should be filled by content area experts who are continuously keeping up with the science and can provide accurate feedback to applicants and makes informed suggestions about health priorities. Many grants are longer than a single administration and so continuity is paramount and leads to the development of new diagnostics, treatments, and cures for conditions that affect nearly 100 million Americans and cost the U.S. economy billions annually. The progress vision researchers have made—from gene therapies that restore sight to AI-driven diagnostics that detect systemic disease through the eye—has only been possible because of the long-term stability, independence, and credibility of federal science agencies, career staff, and the peer-review grant-making process.
