The proposed rule to move federal civil servants to Schedule F will have a dramatic negative impact on scientific research in the U.S. Rigorous science requires extensive planning, execution of research projects and follow-up activities that span years if not decades. To be effective, strategic vision and strong scientific foundations are needed at the agencies that fund and regulate our research. Politicizing the appointments of leaders in our flagship scientific institutions will be destructive for biomedical progress, waste the time and resources of the research community, and lead to confusion about scientific fundamentals within the American public.

Therefore, as a member of the vision research community and a supporter of evidence-based public policy, I strongly oppose the proposed rule that would enable the reclassification of federal employees into a new Schedule F category. Introducing political instability through Schedule F would:
•	Undermine the merit-based grant review process at NIH, including NEI;
•	Threaten evidence-based policymaking in public health and scientific agencies;
•	Disrupt career pathways for young scientists entering government service;
•	Cause significant staff attrition and institutional knowledge loss at federal agencies.

As a member of the vision research community and a strong advocate for evidence-based public policy, I write to express my firm opposition to the proposed rule that would reclassify federal employees under a new Schedule F category.

This rule poses a serious threat to the integrity and continuity of scientific research across the federal government—including at the National Eye Institute (NEI), where nonpartisan, expert leadership is crucial for advancing breakthroughs in vision science and safeguarding the eye health of millions of Americans. Reclassifying career scientists, grant program officers, and policy experts into Schedule F would make it easier to remove qualified professionals for political reasons and replace them with partisan appointees in roles that should be governed solely by scientific merit and continuity across administrations.

Vision research is a nonpartisan national priority. It drives the development of new diagnostics, treatments, and cures for conditions that impact nearly 100 million Americans and cost the U.S. economy billions each year. The remarkable progress in the field—from gene therapies that restore vision to AI-based tools that detect systemic disease through the eye—has been made possible by the long-term stability, independence, and credibility of federal science agencies, career staff, and the peer-reviewed grant-making process.

Implementing Schedule F would introduce harmful political instability, leading to:

    Erosion of the merit-based grant review process at NIH, including NEI;

    Undermining of evidence-based policymaking in health and science;

    Disruption of career pathways for young scientists in public service;

    Loss of experienced staff and institutional knowledge at federal agencies.

Scientific progress depends on stability, transparency, and nonpartisan leadership. Schedule F would do the opposite—fueling turnover, eroding scientific integrity, and injecting politics into spaces where objective expertise must prevail.

I respectfully urge you to withdraw the proposal to implement Schedule F for NIH and other science-based agencies, in order to protect the integrity and effectiveness of the nation’s scientific workforce.

